\]

The relative speed of the train relative to the jogger is the difference of their speeds since they are moving in the same direction:
\[
45 \text{ m/s} - 9 \text{ m/h} = 36 \text{ m/s}
\]

The train is 150 meters ahead of the jogger when it starts passing the jogger. The time it takes for the train to pass the jogger is 25 seconds.

The distance the train travels to pass the jogger is the sum of the length of the train and the initial distance of the jogger to the end of the train. Since the train is 150 meters ahead of the jogger, the distance the train needs to cover to pass the train is the length of the train plus the initial 150 meters.

The distance the train travels to pass the jogger is the product of the relative speed and the time it takes to pass the jogger:
\[
\text{Distance} = \text{Relative speed} \times \text{Time} = 36 \text{ m/s} \times 25 \text{ s} = 900 \text{ m}
\]

This distance is the length of the train plus the initial 150 meters from the end of the train to the jogger. Therefore, the length of the train is:
\[
L = 900 \text{ m} - 150 \text{ m} = 750 \text{ meters}
\]

So, the length of the train is $\boxed{750}$  meters.
